1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,025
Chapter 62: Connective Tissue Disorders.

3
00:00:06,025 --> 00:00:28,362
Immune-Mediated Connective Tissue Diseases Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Antiphospholipid Syndrome Rheumatoid Arthritis Systemic Sclerosis—Scleroderma Vasculitis Syndromes Inflammatory Myopathies Hereditary Connective Tissue Disorders References Connective tissue disorders, also termed collagen vascular disorders, have two basic underlying causes.

4
00:00:28,362 --> 00:00:35,687
First are the immune-complex diseases, in which deposition of immune complexes causes connective tissue damage.

5
00:00:35,687 --> 00:00:45,650
Because these manifest by sterile inflammation—predominantly of the skin, joints, blood vessels, and kidneys—they are referred to as rheumatic diseases.

6
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:55,800
Many of these immune-complex diseases are more prevalent in women, and systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and rheumatoid arthritis are examples.

7
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:03,400
Second are the inherited disorders that involve bone, skin, cartilage, blood vessels, and basement membranes.

8
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:09,737
These include Marfan syndrome, osteogenesis imperfecta, and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.

9
00:01:09,737 --> 00:01:13,025
Immune-Mediated Connective Tissue Diseases.

10
00:01:13,025 --> 00:01:19,675
These disorders can be separated into those associated with and those without autoantibody formation.

11
00:01:19,675 --> 00:01:37,600
The rheumatoid factor is an autoantibody found in many autoimmune inflammatory conditions such as SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis (scleroderma), mixed connective tissue disease, dermatomyositis, polymyositis, and various vasculitis syndromes.

12
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:49,262
Instead, the RF-seronegative spondyloarthropathies are strongly associated with expression of the antigen termed human leukocyte antigen B27 (HLA-B27).

13
00:01:49,262 --> 00:01:56,975
These include ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic arthritis, Reiter disease, and other arthritis syndromes.

14
00:01:56,975 --> 00:02:07,262
The normal immunosuppression of pregnancy that allows and is essential for successful engraftment of fetal and placental tissues may mitigate activity in some of these syndromes.

15
00:02:07,262 --> 00:02:19,137
First, estrogens upregulate and androgens downregulate T-cell response, and progesterone is immunosuppressive (Abdul Hussain, 2020; Robinson, 2012).

16
00:02:19,137 --> 00:02:27,062
Second, pregnancy induces a predominance of T2 helper cells compared with cytokine-producing T1 helper cells .

17
00:02:27,062 --> 00:02:32,312
These changes are discussed in detail in Chapter 4 (p. 61).

18
00:02:32,312 --> 00:02:38,225
However, active immune-mediated disease may contribute to obstetrical complications .

19
00:02:38,225 --> 00:02:49,325
One longitudinal cohort study found that unrecognized autoimmune systemic rheumatic disorders are associated with significant risk for preeclampsia and fetal-growth restriction .

20
00:02:49,325 --> 00:03:02,425
In this study, the prevalence of unrecognized systemic rheumatic diseases approximated 0.3 percent, and preeclampsia or fetal-growth restriction complicated 25 percent of these pregnancies.

21
00:03:02,425 --> 00:03:09,600
Last, some immune-mediated diseases may be either caused or activated as a result of prior pregnancies.

22
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:20,725
To explain, fetal cells and cell-free DNA are detectable in maternal blood beginning early in pregnancy (Simpson, 2013; Waldorf, 2008).

23
00:03:20,725 --> 00:03:28,300
Fetal cell microchimerism is the persistence of fetal cells in the maternal circulation and in organs following pregnancy.

24
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:35,087
These fetal cells may become engrafted in maternal tissues and stimulate production of autoantibodies.

25
00:03:35,087 --> 00:03:47,500
Evidence for this includes fetal stem cells engrafted in tissues in women with autoimmune thyroiditis and systemic sclerosis (Jimenez, 2005; Srivatsa, 2001).

26
00:03:47,500 --> 00:04:00,275
Such microchimerism has also been described in women with SLE and those with rheumatoid arthritis– associated HLA alleles (da Silva, 2016; Lee, 2010).

27
00:04:00,275 --> 00:04:10,487
Conversely, engrafted maternal cells may provoke autoimmune conditions in a woman’s offspring (Stevens, 2016; Ye, 2012).

28
00:04:10,487 --> 00:04:13,525
Systemic Lupus Erythematosus.

29
00:04:13,525 --> 00:04:22,437
This autoimmune disease has an intricate pathogenesis that results from interactions between susceptibility genes and environmental factors .

30
00:04:22,437 --> 00:04:31,025
Tissue-binding autoantibodies and immune complexes that are directed at one or more nuclear components lead to cellular and organ damage .

31
00:04:31,025 --> 00:04:38,912
In addition to this B-cell antibody production, immunosuppression is impaired, including regulatory T-cell function .

32
00:04:38,912 --> 00:04:45,225
Some autoantibodies produced in patients with SLE are shown in Table 62-1.

33
00:04:45,225 --> 00:04:49,400
Most patients have autoantibodies for many years before

34
00:04:49,564 --> 00:04:52,214
clinical disease manifests.

35
00:04:52,164 --> 00:04:54,814
TABLE 62-1.

36
00:04:54,814 --> 00:05:09,651
Some Autoantibodies Produced in Patients with Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Genetic influences are implicated, and the concordance is higher with monozygotic compared with dizygotic twins—25 versus 2 percent, respectively.

37
00:05:09,651 --> 00:05:15,376
Moreover, the frequency in patients with one affected family member is 10 percent.

38
00:05:15,376 --> 00:05:27,301
The relative risk of disease rises if the “autoimmunity gene” on chromosome 16 that predisposes to SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, Crohn disease, and psoriasis is inherited.

39
00:05:27,301 --> 00:05:43,576
However, susceptibility genes such as HLA-DR3, STAT4, APOL1, FCGR3A, and DNAse1 explain only a portion of the genetic heritability (Hahn, 2018; Yang, 2013).

40
00:05:43,576 --> 00:05:50,376
Interestingly, maternal exposure to fetal genes elevates susceptibility to SLE development.

41
00:05:50,376 --> 00:05:57,826
A case-control study found that a child’s HLA-DRB1 genotype raises the risk of SLE in the mother .

42
00:05:57,826 --> 00:06:09,139
In contrast, neonatal lupus has been reported in an infant conceived via oocyte donor to a mother with autoimmune disease with circulating anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies .

43
00:06:09,139 --> 00:06:13,151
# Clinical Manifestations and Diagnosis.

44
00:06:13,151 --> 00:06:26,189
Almost 90 percent of SLE cases develop in women, and it more commonly affects African-American women compared with other populations (Izmirly, 2017; Stojan, 2018).

45
00:06:26,189 --> 00:06:31,576
Its prevalence in those of childbearing age approximates 1 case in 500 .

46
00:06:31,576 --> 00:06:36,889
Accordingly, the disease is encountered relatively frequently during pregnancy.

47
00:06:36,889 --> 00:06:42,201
SLE notoriously varies in its presentation, course, and outcome .

48
00:06:42,201 --> 00:06:48,264
Findings may be confined initially to one organ system, and others become involved later.

49
00:06:48,264 --> 00:06:52,101
Instead, the disease may first be multisystem.

50
00:06:52,101 --> 00:07:01,989
Frequent findings are malaise, fever, arthritis, rash, pleuropericarditis, photosensitivity, anemia, and cognitive dysfunction.

51
00:07:01,989 --> 00:07:05,489
At least half of patients have renal involvement.

52
00:07:05,489 --> 00:07:14,751
SLE is also associated with declines in attention, memory, and reasoning (Hahn, 2018; Kozora, 2008).

53
00:07:14,751 --> 00:07:17,426
Table 62-3.

54
00:07:17,426 --> 00:07:31,401
Clinical Criteria for Classification of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Identification of antinuclear antibodies (ANA) is the best screening test, however, a positive result is not specific for SLE.

55
00:07:31,401 --> 00:07:40,751
Low titers can be found in normal individuals, other autoimmune diseases, acute viral infections, and chronic inflammatory processes.

56
00:07:40,751 --> 00:07:44,589
Several drugs also can cause a positive reaction.

57
00:07:44,589 --> 00:07:55,414
Antibodies to double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) and to Smith (Sm) antigens are relatively specific for SLE, whereas other antibodies are not .

58
00:07:55,414 --> 00:08:03,064
Although hundreds of autoantibodies have been described in SLE, only a few have been shown to participate in tissue injury .

59
00:08:03,064 --> 00:08:08,089
Microarray profiles are being developed for more accurate diagnosis .

60
00:08:08,089 --> 00:08:14,076
Chronic anemia develops frequently, and there may be leukopenia and thrombocytopenia.

61
00:08:14,076 --> 00:08:19,151
Proteinuria and casts are found in the half of patients with glomerular lesions.

62
00:08:19,151 --> 00:08:26,614
Lupus nephritis can also cause renal insufficiency, which is more common if there are antiphospholipid antibodies .

63
00:08:26,614 --> 00:08:36,064
Other laboratory findings include false-positive syphilis serology, prolonged partial thromboplastin time, and higher rheumatoid factor levels.

64
00:08:36,064 --> 00:08:46,626
Increased serum D-dimer concentrations often follow a flare or infection, but unexplained persistent elevations are associated with a high risk for thrombosis .

65
00:08:46,626 --> 00:08:52,239
The diagnostic criteria for SLE are listed in Table 62-3.

66
00:08:52,239 --> 00:09:00,239
If any four or more of the 11 clinical criteria are present, serially or simultaneously, the diagnosis is made.

67
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:04,714
Importantly, numerous drugs can induce a lupus-like syndrome.

68
00:09:04,714 --> 00:09:15,126
These include proton-pump inhibitors, thiazide diuretics, antifungals, antihypertensives, chemotherapeutics, statins, and antiepileptics.

69
00:09:15,126 --> 00:09:22,989
Drug-induced lupus is rarely associated with glomerulonephritis and usually regresses when the medication is discontinued .

70
00:09:22,989 --> 00:09:25,564
Table 62-2.

71
00:09:25,564 --> 00:09:32,076
Some Clinical Manifestations of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus # Lupus and Pregnancy.

72
00:09:32,076 --> 00:09:40,076
In a metaanalysis of nearly 9 million pregnancies, the incidence of lupus approximated 1 case in 900 births .

73
00:09:40,076 --> 00:09:47,764
During pregnancy, lupus improves in a third of women, remains unchanged in a third, and worsens in the remaining third.

74
00:09:47,764 --> 00:09:53,264
Thus, in any pregnancy, the clinical condition can worsen or flare without

75
00:09:53,428 --> 00:09:54,878
warning .

76
00:09:54,828 --> 00:10:04,990
In a cohort of 13,555 pregnant women with SLE, the maternal mortality rate was 325 per 100,000 .

77
00:10:04,990 --> 00:10:09,303
Maternal deaths invariably occur in those with active disease .

78
00:10:09,303 --> 00:10:17,715
Women with SLE are at greater risk for thrombotic complications, infection, postpartum hemorrhage, and blood transfusion.

79
00:10:17,715 --> 00:10:26,653
He and Wei (2020) reported increased risks of adverse pregnancy outcomes in women with SLE compared with unaffected women .

80
00:10:26,653 --> 00:10:29,303
Table 62-4.

81
00:10:29,303 --> 00:10:40,715
Risk of Adverse Pregnancy Outcomes in Women with Systemic Lupus Erythematosus During the past several decades, pregnancy outcomes in women with SLE have improved remarkably.

82
00:10:40,715 --> 00:10:48,440
For most women with inactive or mild/moderate disease activity, pregnancy outcomes are relatively favorable .

83
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:53,715
Women who have confined cutaneous lupus do not usually have adverse outcomes .

84
00:10:53,715 --> 00:10:58,940
However, newly diagnosed SLE during pregnancy tends to be severe .

85
00:10:58,940 --> 00:11:27,815
In general, pregnancy outcome is best in women for whom: (1) lupus activity has been quiescent for at least 6 months before conception; (2) lupus nephritis manifest by proteinuria or renal dysfunction is absent; (3) neither antiphospholipid syndrome nor lupus anticoagulant are detected; and (4) superimposed preeclampsia does not develop (Peart, 2014; Petri, 2020a; Stojan, 2012).

86
00:11:27,815 --> 00:11:30,415
# Lupus Nephritis.

87
00:11:30,415 --> 00:11:43,490
Active nephritis is associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes, although these have improved substantially and especially if disease remains in remission (Stojan, 2012; Wagner, 2009).

88
00:11:43,490 --> 00:11:50,915
Of complications, women with renal disease have a high incidence of gestational hypertension and preeclampsia.

89
00:11:50,915 --> 00:12:06,765
Of 137 women with SLE reported by Rodrigues and colleagues (2019), 27 percent of gravidas with preexisting renal disease developed preeclampsia compared with only 16 percent of those without underlying renal disease.

90
00:12:06,765 --> 00:12:17,478
In a review of 309 pregnancies complicated by lupus nephritis, 30 percent suffered a flare, and 40 percent of these had associated renal insufficiency .

91
00:12:17,478 --> 00:12:21,403
The maternal mortality rate was 1.3 percent.

92
00:12:21,403 --> 00:12:25,965
These findings were corroborated in a subsequent prospective study .

93
00:12:25,965 --> 00:12:31,265
In addition, a third of the 113 pregnancies were delivered preterm.

94
00:12:31,265 --> 00:12:45,515
Compared with women without nephritis or with inactive nephritis, those with active nephritis have a significantly increased incidence of maternal and fetal complications (Rodrigues, 2019; Wagner, 2009).

95
00:12:45,515 --> 00:12:49,465
The higher the histological class, the greater the risk.

96
00:12:49,465 --> 00:12:55,403
Most recommend continuation of immunosuppressive therapy for nephritis during pregnancy.

97
00:12:55,403 --> 00:13:08,553
Hydroxychloroquine, cyclosporine, azathioprine, and tacrolimus are permissible in pregnancy (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b; Parikh, 2020).

98
00:13:08,553 --> 00:13:27,365
New-onset nephritis or severe renal flare is treated aggressively with tacrolimus, and consideration is given to adding intravenous corticosteroids, immunosuppressive drugs, or intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) therapy (Maynard, 2019; Parikh, 2020).

99
00:13:27,365 --> 00:13:31,190
# Lupus Versus Preeclampsia-Eclampsia.

100
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:37,240
Chronic hypertension complicates up to 30 percent of pregnancies in women with SLE .

101
00:13:37,240 --> 00:13:50,978
Preeclampsia is common, and in those with nephritis or antiphospholipid antibodies, superimposed preeclampsia is encountered even more often and earlier (Bertsias, 2008; Dong, 2020).

102
00:13:50,978 --> 00:13:59,415
Preeclampsia and SLE flares share features of hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and renal function deterioration.

103
00:13:59,415 --> 00:14:02,478
However, the management is distinct.

104
00:14:02,478 --> 00:14:09,965
Lupus nephritis is treated with immunosuppression, and severe preeclampsia/eclampsia requires delivery.

105
00:14:09,965 --> 00:14:19,653
It may be difficult, if not impossible, to discriminate a lupus flare with nephropathy from severe preeclampsia if the kidney is the only involved organ .

106
00:14:19,653 --> 00:14:26,390
Serum uric acid and complement levels and dsDNA titers may aid in differentiating the two.

107
00:14:26,390 --> 00:14:33,915
Last, central nervous system involvement with SLE may culminate in convulsions similar to those of eclampsia.

108
00:14:33,915 --> 00:14:36,978
# Management During Pregnancy.

109
00:14:36,978 --> 00:14:43,628
Care consists primarily of monitoring maternal clinical and laboratory status and fetal well-being.

110
00:14:43,628 --> 00:14:54,428
Establishment of baseline renal disease and hematological values is important because pregnancy-induced laboratory changes later in gestation can resemble SLE disease

111
00:14:54,580 --> 00:14:56,267
activity.

112
00:14:56,217 --> 00:15:04,105
A 24-hour urine collection to assess proteinuria and serum creatinine level measurement determine initial renal function.

113
00:15:04,105 --> 00:15:11,805
A complete blood count (CBC) identifies preexisting anemia, leukopenia, and thrombocytopenia.

114
00:15:11,805 --> 00:15:19,580
Continued assessment of renal function and hematological parameters is undertaken each trimester unless symptoms develop.

115
00:15:19,580 --> 00:15:22,517
In this event, measures are monthly .

116
00:15:22,517 --> 00:15:28,530
For monitoring of SLE activity, various laboratory techniques have been recommended.

117
00:15:28,530 --> 00:15:34,867
As just discussed, establishing baseline values will assist comparisons later in pregnancy.

118
00:15:34,867 --> 00:15:44,017
Serum complement levels (C3 and C4) and dsDNA titers are drawn at the first prenatal care visit and when symptoms develop.

119
00:15:44,017 --> 00:15:50,542
Serum complement levels are normally increased in pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1231).

120
00:15:50,542 --> 00:16:00,242
Although falling or low C3 and C4 levels are more likely to be associated with active disease, higher levels provide no assurance against disease flare.

121
00:16:00,242 --> 00:16:08,517
If symptoms develop, the combination of declining complement levels and rising dsDNA titers does indicate a lupus flare .

122
00:16:08,517 --> 00:16:15,017
Women with SLE are also screened for antiphospholipid antibodies at the beginning of pregnancy.

123
00:16:15,017 --> 00:16:26,517
Last, screening for anti-SS-A (antiRo) and anti-SS-B (anti-La) antibodies is recommended because of associated fetal complications described later.

124
00:16:26,517 --> 00:16:34,330
The combination of serial hematological, rheumatological, and renal studies may detect changes in disease activity.

125
00:16:34,330 --> 00:16:44,655
SLE-associated hemolysis is characterized by a positive Coombs test, anemia, reticulocytosis, and unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.

126
00:16:44,655 --> 00:16:48,992
Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, or both may develop.

127
00:16:48,992 --> 00:16:55,205
Chronic thrombocytopenia in early pregnancy may be due to antiphospholipid antibodies .

128
00:16:55,205 --> 00:16:59,605
Later, thrombocytopenia may indicate preeclampsia.

129
00:16:59,605 --> 00:17:06,430
The fetus also is closely observed for adverse effects such as growth restriction and oligohydramnios.

130
00:17:06,430 --> 00:17:13,942
Sonographic evaluations are performed every 4 weeks beginning at 24 weeks’ gestation to assess for both.

131
00:17:13,942 --> 00:17:27,667
Antepartum testing for fetal well-being is initiated in the third trimester and described in Chapter 20 (p. 392) (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021a,b).

132
00:17:27,667 --> 00:17:34,217
Unless evidence of maternal or fetal compromise develops, pregnancy is allowed to progress to term.

133
00:17:34,217 --> 00:17:37,230
# Pharmacological Treatment.

134
00:17:37,230 --> 00:17:41,917
There is no cure for SLE, and complete remissions are rare.

135
00:17:41,917 --> 00:17:50,105
Approximately a fourth of pregnant women have mild disease, which is not life threatening but may be disabling because of pain and fatigue.

136
00:17:50,105 --> 00:17:56,755
Arthralgia and serositis can be managed by nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).

137
00:17:56,755 --> 00:18:06,367
However, during pregnancy, chronic or large intermittent dosing is avoided due to related oligohydramnios or ductus arteriosus closure .

138
00:18:06,367 --> 00:18:11,167
Antimalarials reduce dermatitis, arthritis, and fatigue .

139
00:18:11,167 --> 00:18:19,580
Although these agents cross the placenta, hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) does not appear to cause congenital malformations .

140
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:30,105
Most recommend continuation during pregnancy because therapy interruption can precipitate a flare (Marder, 2019; Petri, 2019).

141
00:18:30,105 --> 00:18:43,555
For those not previously using hydroxychloroquine, others favor initiation in the first trimester because its use is associated with an 85-percent reduction in the risk of having a small-for-gestational-age neonate .

142
00:18:43,555 --> 00:18:51,292
Also, it lowers the risk of fetal congenital heart block by 50 percent in women with anti-SSA antibodies.

143
00:18:51,292 --> 00:18:54,980
Low-dose aspirin can be used throughout gestation.

144
00:18:54,980 --> 00:19:09,292
Severe disease is managed with corticosteroids such as prednisone, 1 to 2 mg/kg/d orally or methylprednisolone, 1000 mg given intravenously over 90 minutes daily for 3 days .

145
00:19:09,292 --> 00:19:19,617
After the disease is controlled, this dose is tapered to a daily morning dose of 10 to 15 mg. Corticosteroid therapy can lead to gestational diabetes.

146
00:19:19,617 --> 00:19:29,605
During labor, delivery, and postpartum, corticosteroids in “stress doses” are given to women who take these drugs chronically or who have done so recently.

147
00:19:29,605 --> 00:19:41,855
This augmented dose reflects a normal adrenal response, and one option on admission for delivery is hydrocortisone, 100 mg, given IV every 8 hours for 48 hours.

148
00:19:41,855 --> 00:19:48,492
Tacrolimus, rituximab, and IVIG therapy also have been used to treat lupus flares .

149
00:19:48,492 --> 00:19:50,105
These are

150
00:19:50,260 --> 00:19:55,910
usually reserved for lupus nephritis or disease that is corticosteroid resistant.

151
00:19:55,860 --> 00:20:03,710
Azathioprine has a good safety record during pregnancy (Hahn, 2018; Petri, 2019).

152
00:20:03,710 --> 00:20:08,335
Its recommended daily oral dose is 2 mg/kg.

153
00:20:08,335 --> 00:20:19,347
Teratogenic medications to be avoided include mycophenolate mofetil, methotrexate, and cyclophosphamide (Briggs, 2022; Hahn, 2018).

154
00:20:19,347 --> 00:20:25,747
However, cyclophosphamide can be considered in the second or third trimester for severe disease .

155
00:20:25,747 --> 00:20:36,697
In nonpregnant subjects, antihypertensive therapy often includes an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor or an angiotensin-receptor blocker.

156
00:20:36,697 --> 00:20:45,097
For pregnancy, these should be changed to safer fetal options such as calcium-channel blockers, alpha methyldopa, or labetalol .

157
00:20:45,097 --> 00:20:48,647
# Perinatal Mortality and Morbidity.

158
00:20:48,647 --> 00:20:55,335
Adverse perinatal outcome rates are significantly elevated in pregnancies complicated by SLE.

159
00:20:55,335 --> 00:21:05,772
Among these are preterm delivery, fetal-growth restriction, stillbirth, and neonatal lupus syndrome (He, 2020; Phansenee, 2018).

160
00:21:05,772 --> 00:21:16,860
Perinatal outcome rates worsen in women with active disease, significant proteinuria, or renal impairment and in those with chronic hypertension, preeclampsia, or both .

161
00:21:16,860 --> 00:21:24,472
Adverse outcomes are also more common in women with neuropsychiatric lupus (de Jesus, 2017).

162
00:21:24,472 --> 00:21:33,860
Reasons at least partially responsible for adverse fetal consequences include decidual vasculopathy with placental infarction and decreased perfusion .

163
00:21:33,860 --> 00:21:36,797
# Neonatal Lupus Syndrome.

164
00:21:36,797 --> 00:21:46,972
This is characterized by newborn skin lesions—lupus dermatitis; various hematological and systemic derangements; and occasionally congenital heart block .

165
00:21:46,972 --> 00:21:54,447
Cutaneous manifestations can be present in 30 to 40 percent of infants and appear at 4 to 6 weeks of age .

166
00:21:54,447 --> 00:22:04,035
These are usually associated with anti-SS-A and SS-B antibodies, and up to 50 percent of women with SLE are positive for these .

167
00:22:04,035 --> 00:22:10,210
Thrombocytopenia and hepatic involvement are seen in 5 to 10 percent of affected infants.

168
00:22:10,210 --> 00:22:23,847
In a review of outcomes in 128 infants born to women with lupus, 16 percent developed cutaneous lupus and 26 percent showed hematological abnormalities and elevated transaminase levels .

169
00:22:23,847 --> 00:22:31,210
Cutaneous lupus, thrombocytopenia, and autoimmune hemolysis are transient and clear within a few months .

170
00:22:31,210 --> 00:22:37,785
In subsequent offspring, the recurrence risk for neonatal lupus may approach 25 percent .

171
00:22:37,785 --> 00:22:47,610
The only maternal treatment that lowers the risk of neonatal lupus is hydroxychloroquine (Izmirly, 2020; Petri, 2019).

172
00:22:47,610 --> 00:22:50,397
# Congenital Heart Block.

173
00:22:50,397 --> 00:23:00,485
Fetal and neonatal heart block results from diffuse myocarditis and fibrosis in the region between the atrioventricular (AV) node and bundle of His.

174
00:23:00,485 --> 00:23:04,685
Natural killer cells have been detected in fetal myocytes .

175
00:23:04,685 --> 00:23:12,897
Congenital heart block develops almost exclusively in fetuses of women with antibodies to the SS-A or SS-B antigens .

176
00:23:12,897 --> 00:23:23,960
Even in the presence of such antibodies, the incidence of fetal myocarditis is only 2 to 3 percent (Bramham, 2012; Lateef, 2017).

177
00:23:23,960 --> 00:23:28,697
However, it rises to 20 percent with a prior affected child.

178
00:23:28,697 --> 00:23:33,947
The cardiac lesion is permanent, and a pacemaker is generally necessary.

179
00:23:33,947 --> 00:23:36,685
Long-term prognosis is poor.

180
00:23:36,685 --> 00:23:45,385
Of 325 infants with cardiac neonatal lupus, nearly 20 percent died, and of these, a third were stillborn .

181
00:23:45,385 --> 00:23:53,272
Another autoimmune disease, Sjögren syndrome, also is associated with fetal heart block (Brito-Zerón, 2020).

182
00:23:53,272 --> 00:24:02,310
Maternal administration of corticosteroids, plasma exchange, or IVIG therapy does not reduce the risk of congenital heart block.

183
00:24:02,310 --> 00:24:12,072
For women with anti-SS-B and anti-SS-A antibodies, fetal cardiac monitoring is performed between 18 and 26 weeks’ gestation.

184
00:24:12,072 --> 00:24:22,260
Maternal corticosteroid administration for treatment of fetal congenital heart block is controversial and discussed further in Chapter 19 (p. 369).

185
00:24:22,260 --> 00:24:27,622
This therapy to treat fetal heart block has not been studied in randomized trials.

186
00:24:27,622 --> 00:24:38,185
However, some evidence derives from fetuses exposed to their mother’s corticosteroid treatment for SLE and shows that exposure may mitigate fetal myocarditis.

187
00:24:38,185 --> 00:24:51,547
Specifically, Shinohara and coworkers (1999) reported no cases of heart block in 26 neonates whose mothers received corticosteroids before 16 weeks’ gestation as part of SLE

188
00:24:51,700 --> 00:24:53,800
maintenance therapy.

189
00:24:53,750 --> 00:25:04,287
By contrast, 15 of 61 neonates with heart block were born to women in whom corticosteroid therapy was begun after 16 weeks for an SLE exacerbation.

190
00:25:04,287 --> 00:25:16,112
Maternal hydroxychloroquine therapy is associated with a lower incidence of fetal heart block, especially in those with a prior affected child (Izmirly, 2010, 2020).

191
00:25:16,112 --> 00:25:19,950
# Long-term Prognosis and Contraception.

192
00:25:19,950 --> 00:25:29,687
The survival rate for women with SLE is 95 percent at 5 years, 90 percent at 10 years, and 78 percent at 20 years .

193
00:25:29,687 --> 00:25:37,987
Infection, lupus flares, end-organ failure, hypertension, stroke, and cardiovascular disease account for most deaths.

194
00:25:37,987 --> 00:25:49,925
In general, women with SLE and chronic vascular or renal disease may limit family size because of morbidity associated with the disease and greater risks for adverse perinatal outcomes.

195
00:25:49,925 --> 00:26:03,025
For contraception, combination oral contraceptives did not increase the incidence of lupus flares in two large multicenter trials (Petri, 2005; Sánchez-Guerrero, 2005).

196
00:26:03,025 --> 00:26:10,112
Notably, comorbid antiphospholipid antibodies are a contraindication to estrogen-containing methods .

197
00:26:10,112 --> 00:26:17,575
Progestin-only implants and injections provide effective contraception with no known effects on lupus flare rates .

198
00:26:17,575 --> 00:26:26,012
Data do not support the concern that intrauterine device use and immunosuppressive therapy lead to higher infection rates in these patients.

199
00:26:26,012 --> 00:26:33,962
Tubal sterilization may be advantageous and is performed with greatest safety postpartum or whenever SLE is quiescent .

200
00:26:33,962 --> 00:26:36,512
Antiphospholipid Syndrome.

201
00:26:36,512 --> 00:26:44,387
This is an autoantibody-mediated acquired thrombophilia that predisposes to recurrent thrombosis or pregnancy morbidity .

202
00:26:44,387 --> 00:26:59,575
Specifically, antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) is diagnosed in women with persistently positive serum tests for antiphospholipid antibodies (APAs) plus arterial and/or venous thromboses or obstetrical morbidity.

203
00:26:59,575 --> 00:27:07,712
APAs include lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin antibody, and anti-β2 glycoprotein-I antibody.

204
00:27:07,712 --> 00:27:12,962
Phospholipids are the main lipid constituents of cell and organelle membranes.

205
00:27:12,962 --> 00:27:18,200
Certain proteins in plasma associate noncovalently with these phospholipids.

206
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:28,487
APAs are directed against these phospholipids or these phospholipid-binding proteins (Garcia, 2018; Giannakopoulos, 2013).

207
00:27:28,487 --> 00:27:37,237
This antibody group may be of immunoglobulin G (IgG), IgM, and IgA classes, alone or in combination.

208
00:27:37,237 --> 00:27:43,400
APAs are most common with SLE, other connective tissue disorders, and APS.

209
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:49,425
That said, a small proportion of normal women and men have low levels of these antibodies.

210
00:27:49,425 --> 00:27:59,412
The stimulus for autoantibody production is unclear, but it possibly is due to a preceding infection or another event that causes endothelial disruption.

211
00:27:59,412 --> 00:28:17,175
The resulting pathophysiology is mediated by one or more of the following: (1) activation of various procoagulants, (2) inactivation of natural anticoagulants, (3) complement activation, and (4) inhibition of syncytiotrophoblast differentiation .

212
00:28:17,175 --> 00:28:22,900
Clinically, these actions lead to vascular thromboses or to pregnancy morbidity.

213
00:28:22,900 --> 00:28:26,500
Virtually every organ system may be affected.

214
00:28:26,500 --> 00:28:32,162
Central nervous system involvement is one of the most prominent clinical manifestations.

215
00:28:32,162 --> 00:28:41,362
In addition to cerebrovascular arterial or venous thrombotic events, psychiatric features and even multiple sclerosis can be present .

216
00:28:41,362 --> 00:28:50,300
Renovascular involvement may lead to renal failure that can be difficult to differentiate from lupus nephritis (D’Cruz, 2009).

217
00:28:50,300 --> 00:28:54,100
Peripheral and visceral thromboses also are features.

218
00:28:54,100 --> 00:29:01,537
In one case, a puerpera developed a mesenteric vessel infarction and subsequent spontaneous cecal perforation .

219
00:29:01,537 --> 00:29:13,300
Obstetrical complications encompass recurrent pregnancy loss and placental dysfunction reflected by fetal-growth restriction, stillbirth, early-onset preeclampsia, and preterm birth.

220
00:29:13,300 --> 00:29:25,125
Treatment using aspirin, heparin, and close monitoring has increased live-birth rates to more than 70 percent in these women (Hamulyák, 2020; Schreiber, 2016).

221
00:29:25,125 --> 00:29:33,575
A small proportion of these patients develop the catastrophic antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (CAPS) or Asherson syndrome.

222
00:29:33,575 --> 00:29:41,862
This is defined as a rapidly progressive thromboembolic disorder simultaneously affecting three or more organ systems or tissues

223
00:29:42,028 --> 00:29:46,915
Garcia, 2018; Schreiber, 2016).

224
00:29:46,865 --> 00:29:51,490
It has a high mortality rate from activation of a cytokine storm.

225
00:29:51,490 --> 00:29:55,515
In half of cases, a triggering event is identified.

226
00:29:55,515 --> 00:29:59,428
# Specific Antiphospholipid Antibodies.

227
00:29:59,428 --> 00:30:20,053
As mentioned, several antibodies in APS are directed against a specific phospholipid or a phospholipid-binding protein: 1. β2 glycoprotein-I, also known as apolipoprotein H, is a phospholipidbinding plasma protein that inhibits prothrombinase activity within platelets and prevents platelet aggregation .

228
00:30:20,053 --> 00:30:27,540
Thus, its normal action is to limit procoagulant binding and thereby prevent coagulation cascade activation.

229
00:30:27,540 --> 00:30:35,865
Logically, antibodies directed against this glycoprotein would reverse its anticoagulant activity and promote thrombosis.

230
00:30:35,865 --> 00:30:45,240
This is important from an obstetrical viewpoint because β2 glycoprotein-I is expressed in high concentrations on the syncytiotrophoblast surface.

231
00:30:45,240 --> 00:30:53,265
Complement activation may contribute to its pathogenesis (Avalos, 2009; Tsokos, 2011).

232
00:30:53,265 --> 00:31:03,353
Teleologically, this seems appropriate because the decidua intuitively should be a critical area to prevent coagulation that might lead to intervillous space thrombosis.

233
00:31:03,353 --> 00:31:12,153
2. Lupus anticoagulant (LAC) is a heterogeneous group of antibodies directed against phospholipid-binding proteins.

234
00:31:12,153 --> 00:31:20,815
This antibody group induces prolongation in vitro of the prothrombin, partial thromboplastin, and Russell viper venom times.

235
00:31:20,815 --> 00:31:27,915
Thus, paradoxically, this so-called anticoagulant is actually powerfully thrombotic in vivo.

236
00:31:27,915 --> 00:31:33,953
This is the only APA that has been consistently associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes .

237
00:31:33,953 --> 00:31:44,228
3. Anticardiolipin antibodies (ACAs) are directed against one of the many phospholipid cardiolipins found in mitochondrial membranes and platelets.

238
00:31:44,228 --> 00:31:48,440
# Antibodies Against Natural Anticoagulants.

239
00:31:48,440 --> 00:31:54,628
Some APAs are also directed against the natural anticoagulant proteins C and S.

240
00:31:54,628 --> 00:32:03,328
Another is directed against the anticoagulant protein annexin V, which is expressed in high concentrations by syncytiotrophoblast .

241
00:32:03,328 --> 00:32:07,128
Testing for these other antibodies is not recommended .

242
00:32:07,128 --> 00:32:18,778
However, some studies have evaluated these nonconventional antiphospholipid antibodies in women who clinically meet APS criteria but do not have the classic antibody profile.

243
00:32:18,778 --> 00:32:27,203
In one study, treatment of women who had these nonconventional antibodies offered some benefits, such as a lower pregnancy loss rate .

244
00:32:27,203 --> 00:32:33,065
Clinical features shown in Table 62-5 provide indications for testing.

245
00:32:33,065 --> 00:32:42,665
By international consensus, APS is diagnosed based on laboratory and clinical criteria (Table 11-4, p. 205).

246
00:32:42,665 --> 00:32:50,553
First, one of two clinical criteria—which are vascular thrombosis or certain pregnancy morbidity—must be present.

247
00:32:50,553 --> 00:33:03,278
With laboratory criteria, high IgG or IgM titers of ACA or anti-β2 glycoprotein-I or LAC positivity should be confirmed on two occasions 12 weeks apart.

248
00:33:03,278 --> 00:33:08,978
The diagnosis can be further stratified based on the number of these tests that are positive.

249
00:33:08,978 --> 00:33:11,715
Table 62-5.

250
00:33:11,715 --> 00:33:19,128
Some Clinical Features of Antiphospholipid Syndrome Tests for LAC are nonspecific coagulation tests.

251
00:33:19,128 --> 00:33:28,028
The partial thromboplastin time is generally prolonged because the anticoagulant interferes with conversion of prothrombin to thrombin in vitro.

252
00:33:28,028 --> 00:33:32,490
The dilute Russell viper venom test is considered more specific.

253
00:33:32,490 --> 00:33:44,228
Branch and Khamashta (2003) recommend conservative interpretation of results that are based on repeated tests from a reliable laboratory and that are consistent with each clinical case.

254
00:33:44,228 --> 00:33:50,365
Only approximately 20 percent of patients with APS have a positive LAC assay alone.

255
00:33:50,365 --> 00:33:56,965
Thus, ACA enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) testing also should be performed.

256
00:33:56,965 --> 00:34:04,090
Efforts have been made to standardize ACA assays, however, these remain without international standards .

257
00:34:04,090 --> 00:34:11,715
For each APS test, interlaboratory variation can be large, and agreement between commercial kits is poor.

258
00:34:11,715 --> 00:34:15,865
# Pregnancy and Antiphospholipid Antibodies.

259
00:34:15,865 --> 00:34:20,428
As many as 30 percent of patients with SLE have APAs.

260
00:34:20,428 --> 00:34:29,390
In women without autoimmune diseases who have pregnancy complications, the prevalence of positive results with APA testing is 7 percent .

261
00:34:29,390 --> 00:34:36,278
In women with elevated APA levels, and especially when LAC is identified, risks for

262
00:34:36,436 --> 00:34:45,873
decidual vasculopathy, placental infarction, fetal-growth restriction, early-onset preeclampsia, and recurrent fetal death are increased .

263
00:34:45,823 --> 00:35:05,636
Some of these women, like those with SLE, also have a high incidence of venous and arterial thromboses, cerebral thrombosis, hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and pulmonary hypertension (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; Clowse, 2008).

264
00:35:05,636 --> 00:35:17,211
In 191 LAC-negative women with APS, those with ACAs and β2-glycoprotein-I had significantly greater miscarriage rates than if either one alone was positive .

265
00:35:17,211 --> 00:35:25,661
Women with higher titers tend to have more adverse outcomes (Alijotas-Reig, 2020; Gabbay-Benziv, 2018).

266
00:35:25,661 --> 00:35:28,948
# Pregnancy Pathophysiology.

267
00:35:28,948 --> 00:35:35,911
It is not precisely known how APAs cause damage, but it is likely that their actions are multifactorial.

268
00:35:35,911 --> 00:35:45,711
Platelets may be damaged directly by APAs or indirectly by binding β2-glycoprotein-I, which causes platelets to be susceptible to aggregation .

269
00:35:45,711 --> 00:36:03,773
One theory proposes that phospholipid-containing endothelial cell or syncytiotrophoblast membranes may be damaged directly by the APA or indirectly by antibody binding to either β2 glycoprotein-I or annexin V (Rand, 1997, 1998).

270
00:36:03,773 --> 00:36:09,636
This prevents the cell membranes from protecting the syncytiotrophoblast and endothelium.

271
00:36:09,636 --> 00:36:16,061
This exposes the basement membrane, to which damaged platelets can adhere and form a thrombus.

272
00:36:16,061 --> 00:36:24,936
Pierro and colleagues (1999) reported that APAs decreased decidual production of the vasodilating prostaglandin E2.

273
00:36:24,936 --> 00:36:31,611
Diminished protein C or S activity and greater prothrombin activation also may be contributory .

274
00:36:31,611 --> 00:36:37,961
Evidence supports that thrombosis with APS stems from activation of the tissue factor pathway .

275
00:36:37,961 --> 00:36:45,673
Last, uncontrolled placental complement activation by APAs is implicated in fetal loss and growth restriction .

276
00:36:45,673 --> 00:36:51,136
Complications in APS cannot be completely explained by thrombosis alone.

277
00:36:51,136 --> 00:36:56,473
Animal models suggest that effects stem from inflammation rather than thrombosis .

278
00:36:56,473 --> 00:37:05,048
Some investigators hypothesize that APSassociated clotting is triggered as a “second hit” from innate inflammatory immune responses.

279
00:37:05,048 --> 00:37:10,061
These investigators recommend treatment with antiinflammatory agents .

280
00:37:10,061 --> 00:37:13,198
# Adverse Pregnancy Outcomes.

281
00:37:13,198 --> 00:37:18,261
Overall, APAs are associated with higher rates of fetal wastage .

282
00:37:18,261 --> 00:37:26,161
In most early reports that describe these outcomes, however, women were included because they had repeated adverse outcomes.

283
00:37:26,161 --> 00:37:39,323
Both antibody prevalence and miscarriage are common—recall that the incidence of APAs in the general obstetrical population is about 5 percent and early miscarriage approximates 20 percent of call conceptions.

284
00:37:39,323 --> 00:37:47,798
Accordingly, current data are too limited to conclude the exact risks for adverse effects of these antibodies on pregnancy outcomes.

285
00:37:47,798 --> 00:37:58,173
Fetal deaths, however, are more characteristic with APS than are first-trimester miscarriages (Garcia, 2018; Roque, 2004).

286
00:37:58,173 --> 00:38:03,173
When otherwise unexplained fetal deaths are examined, the data are mixed.

287
00:38:03,173 --> 00:38:14,136
One study measured ACA levels in 309 pregnancies with fetal death and found no differences in their frequency compared with levels in 618 normal pregnancies .

288
00:38:14,136 --> 00:38:21,611
In another study of women with recurrent pregnancy loss, those with APAs had an increased rate of preterm delivery .

289
00:38:21,611 --> 00:38:35,711
In a case-control study of 582 stillbirths and 1547 live births, a three- to fivefold higher risk for stillbirth was found in women with elevated ACA and anti-β2 glycoprotein-I levels .

290
00:38:35,711 --> 00:38:51,136
In women with APAs, adverse outcomes are more common in the presence of: (1) all three classic APA types, (2) comorbid SLE or systemic autoimmune diseases, and (3) prior thrombosis and pregnancy morbidity.

291
00:38:51,136 --> 00:39:01,023
Logistic regression found the probability of pregnancy failure was 93 percent with two or more APA types but was 63 percent for those with only one .

292
00:39:01,023 --> 00:39:04,473
# Thrombosis Prevention in Pregnancy.

293
00:39:04,473 --> 00:39:11,473
Because of study heterogeneity, current treatment recommendations for women with APAs can be confusing.

294
00:39:11,473 --> 00:39:14,923
Therapy is directed at thrombosis prevention.

295
00:39:14,923 --> 00:39:21,123
As discussed, APAs are immunoglobulins that may be of G, M, or A classes.

296
00:39:21,123 --> 00:39:28,848
Those directed against the phospholipids (PL) are termed GPL, MPL, and APL, respectively.

297
00:39:28,848 --> 00:39:30,623
During testing,

298
00:39:30,772 --> 00:39:40,534
these are reported as semiquantified phospholipid binding-unit levels and expressed as negative, low-positive, medium-positive, or high-positive .

299
00:39:40,484 --> 00:39:51,484
As discussed in Chapter 55 (p. 979), women with prior thromboembolic events who have APAs are at risk for recurrence in subsequent pregnancies.

300
00:39:51,484 --> 00:39:59,597
For these women, prophylactic anticoagulation with heparin throughout pregnancy and for 6 weeks postpartum is recommended .

301
00:39:59,597 --> 00:40:09,722
For those without prior thromboembolic events, recommendations for management vary and are listed in Table 55-5 (p. 984).

302
00:40:09,722 --> 00:40:22,047
Some acceptable schemes include close antepartum maternal observation with or without prophylactic or intermediate-dose heparin, and then some form of postpartum anticoagulation for 6 weeks.

303
00:40:22,047 --> 00:40:30,359
Sciascia and coworkers (2016) have presented preliminary salutary results with hydroxychloroquine treatment.

304
00:40:30,359 --> 00:40:37,484
This currently is considered investigational (Garcia, 2018; Latino, 2020).

305
00:40:37,484 --> 00:40:44,309
Several researchers have questioned the need for heparin for women with APAs but no history of thrombosis .

306
00:40:44,309 --> 00:41:01,997
Although this is less clear, some recommend that women be treated if they have medium- or highpositive ACA titers or LAC activity and a prior second- or third-trimester fetal death not attributable to other causes (Dizon-Townson, 1998; Lockshin, 1995).

307
00:41:01,997 --> 00:41:10,372
Others report that women with recurrent early pregnancy loss and medium- or high-positive titers of antibodies may benefit from therapy .

308
00:41:10,372 --> 00:41:27,509
Due to the risk of fetal-growth abnormalities and stillbirth, serial sonographic assessment of fetal growth and antepartum testing in the third trimester is recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a, 2021a,b).

309
00:41:27,509 --> 00:41:30,884
# Specific Therapy in Pregnancy.

310
00:41:30,884 --> 00:41:36,934
Other agents are used to treat gravidas that have APS but no prior thromboembolic event.

311
00:41:36,934 --> 00:41:47,634
Aspirin, in doses of 60 to 80 mg orally daily, blocks conversion of arachidonic acid to thromboxane A2 while sparing prostacyclin production.

312
00:41:47,634 --> 00:41:55,659
Thromboxane A2 usually causes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction, and prostacyclin has the opposite effect.

313
00:41:55,659 --> 00:42:02,947
The only major side effect from low-dose aspirin is a slight risk of small-vessel bleeding during surgical procedures.

314
00:42:02,947 --> 00:42:10,847
Low-dose aspirin does not reduce adverse pregnancy outcomes in women who have APAs but who lack the complete APS syndrome .

315
00:42:10,847 --> 00:42:22,209
Thus, its use is recommended for women with SLE or APS (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020b; Rahman, 2020).

316
00:42:22,209 --> 00:42:30,172
Unfractionated heparin is given subcutaneously in dosages of 5000 to 10,000 units every 12 hours.

317
00:42:30,172 --> 00:42:37,384
Some prefer low-molecular-weight heparin, such as 40 mg enoxaparin (Lovenox) once daily .

318
00:42:37,384 --> 00:42:44,872
With therapeutic dosing, measurement of heparin levels may be useful because clotting tests can be altered by LAC.

319
00:42:44,872 --> 00:42:50,884
The rationale for heparin therapy is to prevent venous and arterial thrombotic episodes.

320
00:42:50,884 --> 00:42:58,572
Heparin therapy also prevents thrombosis in the microcirculation, including the decidualtrophoblastic interface .

321
00:42:58,572 --> 00:43:05,297
As discussed, heparin binds to β2glycoprotein-I, which coats the syncytiotrophoblast.

322
00:43:05,297 --> 00:43:14,159
This prevents binding of ACAs and anti-β2 glycoprotein-I antibodies to their surfaces, which likely prevents cellular damage .

323
00:43:14,159 --> 00:43:18,797
Heparin also binds to APAs in vitro and likely in vivo.

324
00:43:18,797 --> 00:43:27,122
Aspirin plus heparin therapy is the most effective regimen (de Jesus, 2014; Hamulyák, 2020).

325
00:43:27,122 --> 00:43:33,759
However, despite treatment, 30 percent of women with APS do not achieve a successful outcome .

326
00:43:33,759 --> 00:43:42,922
Heparin therapy is associated with several complications that include bleeding, thrombocytopenia, osteopenia, and osteoporosis.

327
00:43:42,922 --> 00:43:50,259
A description of various heparins and their adverse effects is found in Chapter 55 (p. 983).

328
00:43:50,259 --> 00:43:58,584
Corticosteroids generally should not be used with primary APS—that is, without an associated connective tissue disorder.

329
00:43:58,584 --> 00:44:06,484
For women with SLE or those being treated for APS who develop SLE, corticosteroid therapy is indicated.

330
00:44:06,484 --> 00:44:15,547
In such cases of secondary APS seen with SLE, the dose of prednisone should be maintained at the lowest effective level to prevent flares.

331
00:44:15,547 --> 00:44:23,959
IVIG therapy is controversial and has usually been reserved for women with overt disease—including catastrophic antiphospholipid

332
00:44:24,124 --> 00:44:28,224
syndrome or heparin-induced thrombocytopenia or both .

333
00:44:28,174 --> 00:44:35,899
It is used when other first-line therapies have failed, especially in the setting of preeclampsia and fetal-growth restriction.

334
00:44:35,899 --> 00:44:45,324
IVIG is administered by some in doses of 0.4 g/kg/d for 5 days—total dose of 2 g/kg.

335
00:44:45,324 --> 00:44:51,599
This is repeated monthly, or it is given as a single dose of 1 g/kg each month.

336
00:44:51,599 --> 00:44:56,711
Treatment is expensive, as one course costs more than $10,000.

337
00:44:56,711 --> 00:45:05,624
Tenti and colleagues (2016) found no benefits from adding IVIG to low-dose aspirin and lowmolecular-weight heparin.

338
00:45:05,624 --> 00:45:13,636
One Cochrane review found no improvement in the live-birth rate for IVIG therapy given to women with recurrent pregnancy loss .

339
00:45:13,636 --> 00:45:19,536
Trials are needed before application of this expensive and cumbersome therapy is recommended.

340
00:45:19,536 --> 00:45:27,036
Immunosuppression with hydroxychloroquine is commonly used with low-dose aspirin in the treatment of women with SLE.

341
00:45:27,036 --> 00:45:39,786
This regimen may be beneficial by reducing the risk of thrombosis and improving pregnancy outcomes in women who also have APS (Mekinian, 2015; Sciascia, 2016).

342
00:45:39,786 --> 00:45:44,686
Statins have been examined due to their protective effects on endothelium.

343
00:45:44,686 --> 00:46:01,186
In a small trial in 21 women with APS who developed fetalgrowth restriction or preeclampsia, the addition of pravastatin to low-dose aspirin and low-molecular-weight heparin improved placental blood flow, preeclampsia features, and pregnancy outcomes .

344
00:46:01,186 --> 00:46:05,311
Larger trials are needed before this therapy is recommended.

345
00:46:05,311 --> 00:46:22,811
Described earlier (p. 1114), catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome is treated aggressively with full anticoagulation, high-dose corticosteroids, plasma exchange, and/or IVIG therapy (Garcia, 2018; Rahman, 2020).

346
00:46:22,811 --> 00:46:25,386
# Treatment Efficacy.

347
00:46:25,386 --> 00:46:32,399
Fetal loss is common in women with untreated APS, and especially in those with LAC positivity.

348
00:46:32,399 --> 00:46:42,824
Even with treatment, recurrent fetal loss rates remain at 20 to 30 percent (Arslan, 2020; Empson, 2005; Ernest, 2011).

349
00:46:42,824 --> 00:46:46,061
Shown in Table 62-6.

350
00:46:46,061 --> 00:46:51,649
are pregnancy outcomes from 750 treated women with primary APS .

351
00:46:51,649 --> 00:46:59,186
Participants were treated with low-dose aspirin and prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin starting in the first trimester.

352
00:46:59,186 --> 00:47:05,936
Importantly, some women with SLE and APAs have normal pregnancy outcomes without treatment.

353
00:47:05,936 --> 00:47:13,786
It is also emphasized that women with LAC and prior poor pregnancy outcomes have had liveborn neonates without treatment.

354
00:47:13,786 --> 00:47:16,474
TABLE 62-6.

355
00:47:16,474 --> 00:47:33,211
Pregnancy Outcomes (%) in 750 Women Treated for Antiphospholipid Syndrome—the PREGNANTS Study In a manner similar to neonatal lupus syndrome (p. 1113), up to 30 percent of newborns demonstrate passively acquired APAs .

356
00:47:33,211 --> 00:47:37,661
One group found higher rates of learning disabilities in these children .

357
00:47:37,661 --> 00:47:42,111
Another reported a fourfold greater risk for perinatal strokes .

358
00:47:42,111 --> 00:47:56,149
Of 141 newborns followed in a European registry, the rate of preterm birth was 16 percent; low birthweight occurred in 17 percent; and later behavioral abnormalities developed in 4 percent of the children.

359
00:47:56,149 --> 00:47:59,649
There were no cases of neonatal thrombosis .

360
00:47:59,649 --> 00:48:08,186
A 7-year study of 36 pregnancies in 26 women who had APS reported three cases of autism spectrum disorder.

361
00:48:08,186 --> 00:48:14,961
All were associated with persistent neonatal anti-β2 glycoprotein-I IgG antibodies .

362
00:48:14,961 --> 00:48:17,261
Rheumatoid Arthritis.

363
00:48:17,261 --> 00:48:29,124
This chronic inflammatory disease stems from immunological dysfunction, and infiltrating T cells secrete cytokines to cause inflammation, polyarthritis, and systemic symptoms.

364
00:48:29,124 --> 00:48:35,036
The cardinal feature is inflammatory synovitis that usually involves the peripheral joints.

365
00:48:35,036 --> 00:48:41,374
The disease has a propensity for cartilage destruction, bony erosions, and joint deformities.

366
00:48:41,374 --> 00:48:46,311
Pain, aggravated by movement, is accompanied by swelling and tenderness.

367
00:48:46,311 --> 00:48:53,736
Extraarticular manifestations include rheumatoid nodules, vasculitis, and pleuropulmonary symptoms.

368
00:48:53,736 --> 00:48:57,974
Other complaints are fatigue, anorexia, and depression.

369
00:48:57,974 --> 00:49:03,186
The American College of Rheumatology has published diagnostic criteria .

370
00:49:03,186 --> 00:49:15,236
The worldwide prevalence of rheumatoid arthritis is 0.5 to 1 percent, women are affected three times more often than men, and peak onset is from 25 to 55 years .

371
00:49:15,236 --> 00:49:21,536
There is a genetic predisposition, and heritability is estimated at 15 to 30 percent .

372
00:49:21,536 --> 00:49:25,286
Genome-wide associated studies have identified

373
00:49:25,444 --> 00:49:32,531
more than 30 loci involved in rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis (Kurkó, 2013).

374
00:49:32,481 --> 00:49:45,706
Class II major histocompatibility complex molecule HLA-DR4 and HLA-DRB1 alleles also show an association (McInnes, 2011; Shah, 2018).

375
00:49:45,706 --> 00:49:57,919
Pregnancy provides a protection against rheumatoid arthritis development, and this may be related to HLAdisparate fetal microchimerism (Förger, 2020; Guthrie, 2010).

376
00:49:57,919 --> 00:50:00,144
# Management.

377
00:50:00,144 --> 00:50:08,319
Treatment is directed at pain relief, inflammation reduction, protection of articular structures, and preservation of function.

378
00:50:08,319 --> 00:50:13,294
Physical and occupational therapy and self-care instructions are essential.

379
00:50:13,294 --> 00:50:27,156
Aspirin and other NSAIDs nonspecifically inhibit both cyclooxygenase 1 (COX-1), which is an enzyme critical to normal platelet function, and COX2, which mediates inflammatory response mechanisms.

380
00:50:27,156 --> 00:50:36,956
However, in one systematic review, a small increased rate of cardiac malformations was found in newborns exposed to NSAIDs in the first trimester .

381
00:50:36,956 --> 00:50:46,431
In addition, NSAIDs are associated with early spontaneous abortions, ductus arteriosus constriction, and neonatal pulmonary hypertension .

382
00:50:46,431 --> 00:50:52,181
Another potential side effect of chronic NSAID therapy is gastritis with acute bleeding.

383
00:50:52,181 --> 00:50:57,231
Thus, risks versus benefits of these medications must be considered.

384
00:50:57,231 --> 00:51:03,531
Glucocorticoid therapy in low to moderate doses is given to achieve more rapid symptom control.

385
00:51:03,531 --> 00:51:13,856
Of these, prednisone, 7.5 mg orally daily for the first 2 years of active disease, substantively reduces progressive joint erosions .

386
00:51:13,856 --> 00:51:20,269
Until recently, NSAIDs were the cornerstone of therapy, but they do not retard disease progression.

387
00:51:20,269 --> 00:51:29,831
The American College of Rheumatology recommends several disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs) that may reduce or prevent joint damage .

388
00:51:29,831 --> 00:51:37,544
According to Shah and St. Clair (2018), methotrexate has become the preferred DMARD outside of pregnancy.

389
00:51:37,544 --> 00:51:48,044
NSAIDs thus are important in pregnancy because methotrexate and leflunomide are contraindicated (Chap. 8, pp. 151–152).

390
00:51:48,044 --> 00:51:55,594
In one review of drug exposure, a fourth of women with rheumatoid arthritis took a DMARD within 6 months of conception .

391
00:51:55,594 --> 00:52:08,081
Of unplanned exposures during pregnancy, 4 percent of 393 pregnant women were given a category D or X medication—methotrexate was the most common at 2.9 percent.

392
00:52:08,081 --> 00:52:17,006
Sulfasalazine and hydroxychloroquine are safe for use in pregnancy (Briggs, 2022; Partlett, 2011).

393
00:52:17,006 --> 00:52:26,069
These, combined with relatively low-dose prednisone—7.5 to 20 mg daily—usually successfully treat flares.

394
00:52:26,069 --> 00:52:31,006
Biological DMARDs have revolutionized rheumatoid arthritis treatment.

395
00:52:31,006 --> 00:52:41,306
These include the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α) inhibitors infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, certolizumab, and etanercept .

396
00:52:41,306 --> 00:52:46,206
Their use in pregnancy is limited, but they appear to be safe for the fetus .

397
00:52:46,206 --> 00:52:56,069
In one review, 13 percent of 393 pregnant women were given a biological cytokine-inhibiting DMARD—primarily etanercept .

398
00:52:56,069 --> 00:53:01,081
In another review of 300 exposures, no fetal effects were noted .

399
00:53:01,081 --> 00:53:05,869
A prospective study of 38 pregnant women found similar results .

400
00:53:05,869 --> 00:53:12,119
In 74 women exposed to adalimumab during pregnancy, no risks were identified .

401
00:53:12,119 --> 00:53:23,294
Little is known regarding the pregnancy effects of anakinra, an interleukin 1 receptor antagonist, or of rituximab, an antagonist to the B-cell CD20 antigen.

402
00:53:23,294 --> 00:53:28,381
Other biologics are abatacept, tocilizumab, and tofacitinib.

403
00:53:28,381 --> 00:53:31,994
# Pregnancy and Rheumatoid Arthritis.

404
00:53:31,994 --> 00:53:41,569
In up to 70 percent of women with rheumatoid arthritis, disease will improve during pregnancy (Raine, 2020; Shah, 2018).

405
00:53:41,569 --> 00:53:48,394
Some studies suggest this may be due to regulatory T-cell alterations (Förger, 2020).

406
00:53:48,394 --> 00:53:53,919
Even so, some women develop disease during pregnancy, and others become worse.

407
00:53:53,919 --> 00:54:05,331
A downside to this respite during pregnancy is that postpartum exacerbation occurs in 40 to 50 percent of women (Eudy, 2018; Jethwa, 2019).

408
00:54:05,331 --> 00:54:11,681
This may stem from postpartum alterations in innate immunity (Häupl, 2008b).

409
00:54:11,681 --> 00:54:18,306
Some studies report a protective effect of pregnancy against developing new-onset rheumatoid arthritis.

410
00:54:18,306 --> 00:54:29,481
In a case-control study of 88 affected women, pregnancy provided a degree of protection long term, but the likelihood of new-onset rheumatoid arthritis was increased sixfold

411
00:54:29,644 --> 00:54:32,706
during the first 3 postpartum months .

412
00:54:32,656 --> 00:54:42,281
Pikwer and colleagues (2009) reported a significant reduction in the risk of subsequent arthritis in women who breastfed longer than 12 months.

413
00:54:42,281 --> 00:54:56,244
These findings may reflect the interference of sex hormones with several putative processes involved in arthritis pathogenesis, including immunoregulation (Förger, 2020; Häupl, 2008a,b).

414
00:54:56,244 --> 00:55:07,556
First, Unger and associates (1983) reported that amelioration of rheumatoid arthritis correlated with serum levels of pregnancy-associated alpha2-glycoprotein.

415
00:55:07,556 --> 00:55:11,119
This compound has immunosuppressive properties.

416
00:55:11,119 --> 00:55:22,194
Second, Nelson and coworkers (1993) noted that disease improvement was associated with a disparity in HLA class II antigens between mother and fetus.

417
00:55:22,194 --> 00:55:30,606
They suggested that the maternal immune response to paternal HLA antigens may play a role in pregnancy-induced remission of arthritis.

418
00:55:30,606 --> 00:55:37,919
In addition to monocyte activations, there also may be T-lymphocyte activation (Förger, 2020).

419
00:55:37,919 --> 00:55:44,894
Fertility is diminished in patients with rheumatoid arthritis, and adverse pregnancy outcomes are increased.

420
00:55:44,894 --> 00:55:53,381
Kishore and colleagues (2019) reported pregnancy outcomes from nearly 32,000 women with rheumatoid arthritis.

421
00:55:53,381 --> 00:56:04,656
Compared with unaffected women, those with rheumatoid arthritis had significantly greater rates of hypertensive diseases, preterm birth, hemorrhage, and fetal-growth restriction.

422
00:56:04,656 --> 00:56:08,044
# Juvenile Rheumatoid Arthritis.

423
00:56:08,044 --> 00:56:14,894
This group of diseases is the most frequent cause of chronic arthritis in children and persists into adulthood.

424
00:56:14,894 --> 00:56:29,631
In 76 pregnancies of 51 affected Norwegian women, pregnancy had no effects on clinical presentation, but disease activity usually became quiescent or remained so during pregnancy (Østensen, 1991).

425
00:56:29,631 --> 00:56:34,244
Similar to rheumatoid arthritis, postpartum flares were common.

426
00:56:34,244 --> 00:56:43,331
Joint deformities often developed in these women, and 15 of 20 cesarean deliveries were done for contracted pelves or joint prostheses.

427
00:56:43,331 --> 00:56:49,556
Results from a summary of 39 Polish women with juvenile rheumatoid arthritis were similar .

428
00:56:49,556 --> 00:56:53,781
This arthritis portends few adverse pregnancy outcomes.

429
00:56:53,781 --> 00:57:04,931
The risk for preterm birth is elevated, but fetal development is normal (Mohamed, 2016; Rom, 2014; Wallenius, 2014).

430
00:57:04,931 --> 00:57:16,669
In a study of 1807 births, Remaeus and associates (2017) reported increased incidences of preterm birth, fetalgrowth restriction, and preeclampsia.

431
00:57:16,669 --> 00:57:24,394
Disease severity in early pregnancy was predictive of preterm delivery and fetal-growth restriction in one cohort study .

432
00:57:24,394 --> 00:57:33,519
And, patients with low disease activity scores in the first trimester are likely to have low disease activity or remission in the third trimester .

433
00:57:33,519 --> 00:57:39,331
If the cervical spine is involved, particular attention is warranted during pregnancy.

434
00:57:39,331 --> 00:57:46,744
Subluxation is common, and pregnancy, at least theoretically, predisposes to this because of joint laxity.

435
00:57:46,744 --> 00:57:52,119
Importantly, there are anesthesia concerns during endotracheal intubation.

436
00:57:52,119 --> 00:57:55,294
Systemic Sclerosis—Scleroderma.

437
00:57:55,294 --> 00:58:00,706
This is a chronic multisystem connective tissue disorder of unknown etiology.

438
00:58:00,706 --> 00:58:13,619
It is characterized by microvascular damage; inflammation from immune system activation; and excessive deposition of collagen in the skin, lungs, heart, gastrointestinal tract, and kidneys.

439
00:58:13,619 --> 00:58:24,681
It is uncommon, displays a 5to-1 female dominance, and typically affects those aged 30 to 50 years (Clark, 2020; Varga, 2018).

440
00:58:24,681 --> 00:58:37,219
This strong prevalence of scleroderma in women and its greater incidence in the years following childbirth give credence to the hypothesis that microchimerism is involved as discussed earlier (p. 1109) .

441
00:58:37,219 --> 00:58:48,094
Artlett and associates (1998) demonstrated Y-chromosomal DNA in almost half of women with systemic sclerosis compared with only 4 percent of controls.

442
00:58:48,094 --> 00:58:59,256
Rak and coworkers (2009) identified male microchimerism in peripheral blood mononuclear cells more frequently in women with limited versus diffuse scleroderma.

443
00:58:59,256 --> 00:59:01,731
# Clinical Course.

444
00:59:01,731 --> 00:59:05,331
The hallmark is overproduction of normal collagen.

445
00:59:05,331 --> 00:59:10,981
Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis is the more benign form and progresses slowly.

446
00:59:10,981 --> 00:59:20,781
With diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis, skin fibrosis advances rapidly and is followed by gastrointestinal tract fibrosis, especially the

447
00:59:20,932 --> 00:59:23,057
distal esophagus .

448
00:59:23,007 --> 00:59:32,344
Pulmonary interstitial fibrosis along with vascular changes may cause pulmonary hypertension, which develops in 15 percent of patients.

449
00:59:32,344 --> 00:59:42,182
Antinuclear antibodies such as those listed in Table 62-1 are found in 95 percent of patients, and immunoincompetence often develops.

450
00:59:42,182 --> 00:59:51,982
Raynaud phenomenon, which is typified by cold-induced, episodic, rapidly reversible digital ischemia, is seen in 95 percent of patients.

451
00:59:51,982 --> 00:59:56,157
There also may be swelling of the distal extremities and face.

452
00:59:56,157 --> 01:00:03,719
Symptoms from esophageal involvement, especially fullness and epigastric burning pain, occur in half of patients.

453
01:00:03,719 --> 01:00:07,707
Pulmonary involvement is frequent and causes dyspnea.

454
01:00:07,707 --> 01:00:21,182
The 10-year cumulative survival rate is 70 percent in those with pulmonary fibrosis, and pulmonary arterial hypertension is the main cause of death (Clark, 2020; Varga, 2018).

455
01:00:21,182 --> 01:00:33,282
Women with limited cutaneous disease such as the CREST syndrome—calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal involvement, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasia—have milder disease.

456
01:00:33,282 --> 01:00:39,519
Overlap syndrome refers to systemic sclerosis with features of other connective tissue disorders.

457
01:00:39,519 --> 01:00:54,607
Mixed connective tissue disease is a term used for the syndrome involving features of SLE, systemic sclerosis, polymyositis, rheumatoid arthritis, and high titers of anti-ribonucleoprotein (RNP) antibodies .

458
01:00:54,607 --> 01:01:02,769
The disorder is also termed undifferentiated connective tissue disease (Radin, 2020; Zucchi, 2020).

459
01:01:02,769 --> 01:01:11,457
Although systemic sclerosis is incurable, treatment directed at end-organ involvement can sometimes relieve symptoms and improve function.

460
01:01:11,457 --> 01:01:15,657
Renal involvement and hypertension are often comorbid.

461
01:01:15,657 --> 01:01:22,532
At times, ACE inhibitors may be required for blood pressure control despite their known teratogenicity.

462
01:01:22,532 --> 01:01:31,907
Obliterative vasculopathy of the renal cortical arteries characterizes scleroderma renal crisis, which develops in up to a fourth of these patients.

463
01:01:31,907 --> 01:01:35,919
This leads to renal failure and malignant hypertension.

464
01:01:35,919 --> 01:01:41,207
Interstitial restrictive lung disease is common and frequently becomes life threatening.

465
01:01:41,207 --> 01:01:46,594
Associated pulmonary hypertension is treated with bosentan or sildenafil .

466
01:01:46,594 --> 01:01:50,194
# Pregnancy and Systemic Sclerosis.

467
01:01:50,194 --> 01:01:56,507
The prevalence of scleroderma in pregnancy approximates 1 case in 22,000 pregnancies .

468
01:01:56,507 --> 01:02:02,394
If baseline function is good, these women usually have stable disease during gestation.

469
01:02:02,394 --> 01:02:08,569
As perhaps expected, pregnancy aggravates dysphagia and reflux esophagitis .

470
01:02:08,569 --> 01:02:14,344
Dysphagia results from loss of esophageal motility due to neuromuscular dysfunction.

471
01:02:14,344 --> 01:02:21,957
A lower amplitude or disappearance of peristaltic waves in the lower two thirds of the esophagus is seen using manometry.

472
01:02:21,957 --> 01:02:28,269
Symptomatic treatment for reflux is described in Chapter 57 (p. 1017).

473
01:02:28,269 --> 01:02:35,482
Women with renal insufficiency and malignant hypertension have a higher incidence of superimposed preeclampsia.

474
01:02:35,482 --> 01:02:41,644
With rapidly worsening renal or cardiac disease, pregnancy termination should be considered.

475
01:02:41,644 --> 01:02:47,669
Renal crisis is uncommon, but it is life threatening, and it does not improve with delivery .

476
01:02:47,669 --> 01:02:55,107
Pulmonary hypertension usually contraindicates pregnancy (Table 52-3, p. 919).

477
01:02:55,107 --> 01:03:04,194
Vaginal delivery may be anticipated, unless the soft tissue thickening wrought by scleroderma produces dystocia requiring cesarean delivery.

478
01:03:04,194 --> 01:03:12,344
Tracheal intubation for general anesthesia has special concerns because of limited ability of these women to open their mouths widely .

479
01:03:12,344 --> 01:03:20,057
Because of esophageal dysfunction, aspiration is also more likely, and neuraxial analgesia is preferable.

480
01:03:20,057 --> 01:03:29,794
Warming the delivery room and intravenous fluids, extra blankets, and socks and gloves are considered to improve impaired circulation from Raynaud phenomenon.

481
01:03:29,794 --> 01:03:39,607
If corticosteroids were used frequently, stress doses of hydrocortisone, described earlier for SLE (p. 1113), are recommended .

482
01:03:39,607 --> 01:03:44,369
Maternal and fetal outcomes correlate with underlying disease severity.

483
01:03:44,369 --> 01:03:51,482
In a review of 214 gravidas with systemic sclerosis, 45 percent had diffuse disease .

484
01:03:51,482 --> 01:03:57,344
Major complications included renal crisis in three and greater rates of preterm birth.

485
01:03:57,344 --> 01:04:07,019
A multicenter study of 109 pregnancies from 25 centers reported increased rates of preterm delivery, fetal-growth restriction, and

486
01:04:07,180 --> 01:04:09,817
very-low-birthweight newborns .

487
01:04:09,767 --> 01:04:18,830
In a review of 307 affected pregnancies, risks for miscarriage, preterm births, and gestational hypertension were increased .

488
01:04:18,830 --> 01:04:27,180
These are likely related to placental abnormalities that include decidual vasculopathy, acute atherosis, and infarcts .

489
01:04:27,180 --> 01:04:36,942
Somewhat related, perinatal outcomes are similar with undifferentiated connective tissue diseases (Radin, 2020; Zucchi, 2020).

490
01:04:36,942 --> 01:04:44,392
Scleroderma may be associated with subfertility (Bernatsky, 2008; Lambe, 2004).

491
01:04:44,392 --> 01:04:50,780
For women who do not choose pregnancy, several reversible contraceptive methods are acceptable.

492
01:04:50,780 --> 01:05:01,942
However, hormonal agents, especially combination oral contraceptives, probably should not be used, especially in women with pulmonary, cardiac, or renal involvement.

493
01:05:01,942 --> 01:05:09,205
Due to the often unrelenting progression of systemic sclerosis, permanent sterilization also is considered.

494
01:05:09,205 --> 01:05:11,642
Vasculitis Syndromes.

495
01:05:11,642 --> 01:05:17,342
Inflammation and damage to blood vessels may be primary or caused by another disease.

496
01:05:17,342 --> 01:05:21,942
Immune-complex deposition is presumed to underlie most cases .

497
01:05:21,942 --> 01:05:26,167
Primary types are listed in Table 62-7 .

498
01:05:26,167 --> 01:05:42,755
Small vessel vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis have antibodies directed against proteins in the cytoplasmic granules of leukocytes—antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA) .

499
01:05:42,755 --> 01:05:49,567
In general, women with a vasculitis syndrome have elevated rates of preterm birth and preeclampsia .

500
01:05:49,567 --> 01:05:52,242
Table 62-7.

501
01:05:52,242 --> 01:05:57,067
Primary Vasculitis Syndromes # Polyarteritis Nodosa.

502
01:05:57,067 --> 01:06:09,167
This necrotizing vasculitis of small and medium-sized arteries is characterized clinically by myalgia, neuropathy, gastrointestinal disorders, hypertension, and renal disease .

503
01:06:09,167 --> 01:06:13,867
A third of cases are associated with hepatitis B antigenemia .

504
01:06:13,867 --> 01:06:20,755
Symptoms are nonspecific, and fever, weight loss, and malaise are present in more than half of cases.

505
01:06:20,755 --> 01:06:27,180
Diagnosis is made by biopsy, and treatment is high-dose prednisone plus cyclophosphamide.

506
01:06:27,180 --> 01:06:35,767
Vasculitis due to hepatitis B antigenemia responds to antivirals, glucocorticosteroids, and plasma exchange .

507
01:06:35,767 --> 01:06:41,530
Only a few reports describe polyarteritis nodosa associated with pregnancy.

508
01:06:41,530 --> 01:06:50,880
Of 12 affected gravidas, polyarteritis first manifested during pregnancy in seven, and it was rapidly fatal by 6 weeks postpartum .

509
01:06:50,880 --> 01:06:55,767
The diagnosis was not made until autopsy in six of the seven women.

510
01:06:55,767 --> 01:07:02,105
Four women continued pregnancy, which resulted in one stillborn and three successful outcomes.

511
01:07:02,105 --> 01:07:06,455
# Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis—Wegener.

512
01:07:06,455 --> 01:07:15,067
Granulomatosis This is a small-vessel necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis affecting the upper and lower respiratory tract and kidney .

513
01:07:15,067 --> 01:07:31,092
Disease frequently includes sinusitis and nasal disease (90 percent); pulmonary infiltrates, cavities, or nodules (85 percent); glomerulonephritis (75 percent); and musculoskeletal lesions (65 percent) .

514
01:07:31,092 --> 01:07:34,567
At least 90 percent have polyangiitis .

515
01:07:34,567 --> 01:07:38,755
It is uncommon and usually encountered after age 50.

516
01:07:38,755 --> 01:07:47,592
Koukoura and associates (2008) reviewed 36 cases in association with pregnancy and found a higher preterm birth rate.

517
01:07:47,592 --> 01:07:55,405
In another report, a second woman had disease-related pneumonitis, but pregnancy did not appear to affect disease activity .

518
01:07:55,405 --> 01:08:03,842
Because subglottic stenosis is found in up to a fourth of patients, the anesthesiology service is ideally consulted antepartum .

519
01:08:03,842 --> 01:08:08,930
Therapeutic options are limited by teratogenicity and fetotoxicity .

520
01:08:08,930 --> 01:08:17,055
Corticosteroids are standard treatment, but azathioprine, cyclosporine, and IVIG therapy may also be used.

521
01:08:17,055 --> 01:08:25,230
For severe disease in the late second or third trimester, cyclophosphamide in combination with prednisolone is highly effective.

522
01:08:25,230 --> 01:08:30,642
Rituximab is also approved for this disorder and appears to be safe for the fetus .

523
01:08:30,642 --> 01:08:33,642
# Takayasu Arteritis.

524
01:08:33,642 --> 01:08:40,280
Also called pulseless disease, this is a chronic inflammatory arteritis affecting large vessels .

525
01:08:40,280 --> 01:08:50,855
Unlike temporal arteritis, which develops almost exclusively after age 55, the onset of Takayasu arteritis is almost always before age 40.

526
01:08:50,855 --> 01:08:59,080
It is associated with abnormal angiography of the upper aorta and its main branches and with upper extremity vascular impairment.

527
01:08:59,080 --> 01:09:03,055
Death usually results from congestive heart failure or

528
01:09:03,220 --> 01:09:05,832
cerebrovascular events.

529
01:09:05,782 --> 01:09:14,332
Computed tomography or magnetic resonance angiography can detect this disorder before the development of severe vascular compromise.

530
01:09:14,332 --> 01:09:21,832
Takayasu arteritis may respond symptomatically to corticosteroid therapy, however, it is not curative.

531
01:09:21,832 --> 01:09:26,545
Surgical bypass or angioplasty improves survival rates.

532
01:09:26,545 --> 01:09:38,870
Comorbid severe renovascular hypertension, cardiac involvement, or pulmonary hypertension worsen pregnancy prognosis (Comarmond, 2020; Singh, 2015).

533
01:09:38,870 --> 01:09:43,345
Hypertension is relatively common and should be carefully controlled.

534
01:09:43,345 --> 01:09:47,582
Blood pressure is most accurately measured in the lower extremity.

535
01:09:47,582 --> 01:09:51,295
Overall, the prognosis for pregnancy is good .

536
01:09:51,295 --> 01:09:58,895
A study of 43 pregnancies in 33 women with Takayasu arteritis reported that half had complications .

537
01:09:58,895 --> 01:10:07,782
Authors described an increased risk of pregnancy-related hypertension and preeclampsia but overall favorable maternal and fetal outcomes.

538
01:10:07,782 --> 01:10:15,932
A review from Comarmond and colleagues (2020) cites a 5-percent life-threatening cardiovascular complication rate.

539
01:10:15,932 --> 01:10:20,720
Involvement of the abdominal aorta portends worse perinatal outcome .

540
01:10:20,720 --> 01:10:27,645
Vaginal delivery is preferred, and epidural analgesia has been advocated for labor and delivery.

541
01:10:27,645 --> 01:10:30,482
# Other Vasculitides.

542
01:10:30,482 --> 01:10:36,795
Behçet disease is rare, and its prevalence approximates 1 case per 100,000 births .

543
01:10:36,795 --> 01:10:46,457
In one series of 298 pregnancies in 94 affected women, rates of miscarriage and smaller babies were higher than those in healthy controls .

544
01:10:46,457 --> 01:10:59,095
Although greater risks for preeclampsia, preterm delivery, and venous thromboembolism are reported, pregnancy is not contraindicated (Elliot, 2019; Merlino, 2020).

545
01:10:59,095 --> 01:11:06,595
Formerly Churg-Strauss vasculitis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis is rare in pregnancy .

546
01:11:06,595 --> 01:11:12,207
One case report described a pregnant woman who responded to IVIG therapy .

547
01:11:12,207 --> 01:11:22,695
Another presented an affected 35-year-old woman at term whose necrotizing vasculitis involved the heart, and she subsequently underwent cardiac transplantation .

548
01:11:22,695 --> 01:11:31,345
Edwards (2015) described one woman who developed postpartum relapses of this vasculitis in each of two pregnancies.

549
01:11:31,345 --> 01:11:35,357
Henoch-Schönlein purpura is uncommon after childhood.

550
01:11:35,357 --> 01:11:43,420
Characteristic signs of the disorder include purpuric rash, arthralgia, abdominal pain, and gastrointestinal bleeding.

551
01:11:43,420 --> 01:11:54,832
In one review of 20 pregnancies complicated by this vasculitis, cutaneous lesions and arthralgias were present in three fourths and in approximately one half, respectively .

552
01:11:54,832 --> 01:12:02,145
HenochSchönlein purpura responds well to corticosteroid therapy, and pregnancy outcomes are generally good .

553
01:12:02,145 --> 01:12:04,795
Inflammatory Myopathies.

554
01:12:04,795 --> 01:12:14,107
These are acquired and potentially treatable causes of skeletal muscle weakness with a prevalence of 14 to 32 cases in 100,000 persons .

555
01:12:14,107 --> 01:12:24,007
Three major groups are polymyositis, dermatomyositis, and inclusion-body myositis, which all present with progressive asymmetrical muscle weakness.

556
01:12:24,007 --> 01:12:36,120
They have a variable association with connective tissue diseases, malignancy, drugs, systemic autoimmune disease such as Crohn disease, and viral, bacterial, and parasitic infections.

557
01:12:36,120 --> 01:12:48,207
Polymyositis is a subacute inflammatory myopathy that is frequently associated with one of the autoimmune connective tissue disorders (Bitencourt, 2020; Munira, 2020).

558
01:12:48,207 --> 01:12:54,457
Dermatomyositis manifests as a characteristic rash accompanying or preceding weakness.

559
01:12:54,457 --> 01:13:01,445
Laboratory findings include elevated muscle enzyme levels in serum and an abnormal electromyogram.

560
01:13:01,445 --> 01:13:11,195
Confirmation is by biopsy, which shows perivascular and perimysial inflammatory infiltrates, vasculitis, and muscle fiber degeneration.

561
01:13:11,195 --> 01:13:17,945
It usually develops alone but can overlap with systemic sclerosis or mixed connective tissue disease.

562
01:13:17,945 --> 01:13:25,245
Prevailing theories suggest that the syndromes are caused by viral infections, autoimmune disorders, or both.

563
01:13:25,245 --> 01:13:33,032
Importantly, approximately 15 percent of adults who develop dermatomyositis have an associated malignant tumor.

564
01:13:33,032 --> 01:13:38,432
The timing of myositis and tumor appearance may be separated by several years.

565
01:13:38,432 --> 01:13:44,520
The most common sites of associated cancer are breast, lung, stomach, and ovary.

566
01:13:44,520 --> 01:13:53,520
The disease usually responds to high-dose corticosteroid therapy, immunosuppressive drugs such as azathioprine or methotrexate, or

567
01:13:53,668 --> 01:13:59,105
IVIG (Dalakas, 2012; Linardaki, 2011).

568
01:13:59,055 --> 01:14:04,205
Experiences in pregnancy are garnered mostly from case series and reviews.

569
01:14:04,205 --> 01:14:23,105
One cohort of 17 gravidas with polymyositis/dermatomyositis had higher rates of hypertension (23 percent), antepartum hemorrhage (11 percent), cesarean delivery (88 percent), and preterm birth (35 percent) compared with unaffected pregnant women .

570
01:14:23,105 --> 01:14:32,318
In another series of 60 women with dermatomyositis and 38 with polymyositis, 80 percent had no adverse effect on their disease.

571
01:14:32,318 --> 01:14:40,180
Similar results have been reported by others (Missumi, 2015; Pinal-Fernandez, 2014).

572
01:14:40,180 --> 01:14:47,568
Pregnancy outcome is related to dermatomyositis activity, and new-onset disease is particularly aggressive .

573
01:14:47,568 --> 01:14:50,768
Hereditary Connective Tissue Disorders.

574
01:14:50,768 --> 01:15:00,605
Numerous inherited mutations involve genes that encode for structural proteins of bone, skin, cartilage, blood vessels, and basement membranes.

575
01:15:00,605 --> 01:15:12,805
Although connective tissues contain many complex macromolecules such as elastin and more than 30 proteoglycans, the most common constituents are fibrillar collagen types I, II, and III.

576
01:15:12,805 --> 01:15:27,205
Various mutations, some recessively and some dominantly inherited, result in clinical syndromes that include Marfan and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, osteogenesis imperfecta, chondrodysplasias, and epidermolysis bulla.

577
01:15:27,205 --> 01:15:33,743
Of concern during pregnancy is the predilection for these disorders to result in aortic aneurysms .

578
01:15:33,743 --> 01:15:36,343
# Marfan Syndrome.

579
01:15:36,343 --> 01:15:44,293
This is an autosomal dominant connective tissue disorder that has a population prevalence of 1 case in 3000 to 5000 .

580
01:15:44,293 --> 01:15:47,968
Marfan syndrome affects both sexes equally.

581
01:15:47,968 --> 01:15:56,968
The syndrome is due to abnormal fibrillin—a constituent of elastin—caused by any of several different mutations in the FBN1 gene .

582
01:15:56,968 --> 01:16:03,730
Located on chromosome 15q21, the FBN1 gene has a high mutation rate.

583
01:16:03,730 --> 01:16:12,755
The lack of distinct genotypephenotype correlation and large clinical variability limits the ability to predict disease severity in progeny.

584
01:16:12,755 --> 01:16:23,193
Currently, preimplantation genetic testing and prenatal diagnosis are limited to the 80 percent of cases in which the mutation in the FBN1 gene is known .

585
01:16:23,193 --> 01:16:29,243
In severe disease, the elastic lamina in the media of the aorta shows degeneration.

586
01:16:29,243 --> 01:16:40,155
This predisposes to aortic dilation or dissecting aneurysm, which appears more commonly during pregnancy (Curry, 2014; Roman, 2016).

587
01:16:40,155 --> 01:16:47,693
Marfan syndrome was found to underlie 65 percent of cases of aortic dissection in 27 pregnant women .

588
01:16:47,693 --> 01:16:55,443
Marfan syndrome complicating pregnancy is discussed in more detail in Chapter 52 (p. 936).

589
01:16:55,443 --> 01:16:58,255
# Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome.

590
01:16:58,255 --> 01:17:05,180
This disease is characterized by various connective tissue changes, including skin hyperelasticity.

591
01:17:05,180 --> 01:17:09,430
Its prevalence is 7 cases per 100,000 births .

592
01:17:09,430 --> 01:17:15,068
Several disease types vary based on skin, joint, or other tissue involvement.

593
01:17:15,068 --> 01:17:21,068
In the more severe types, rupture of any of several arteries can cause either stroke or bleeding.

594
01:17:21,068 --> 01:17:26,005
Some types are autosomal dominant, some recessive, and some X-linked .

595
01:17:26,005 --> 01:17:33,568
Types I, II, and III are autosomally dominant, and each accounts for approximately 30 percent of cases.

596
01:17:33,568 --> 01:17:42,355
Type IV is uncommon but is known to predispose to preterm delivery, great-vessel rupture, postpartum bleeding, and uterine rupture .

597
01:17:42,355 --> 01:17:47,843
In most, the underlying molecular defect affects collagen or procollagen.

598
01:17:47,843 --> 01:18:04,768
In general, women with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome have a higher frequency of preterm rupture of membranes, preterm delivery, antepartum and postpartum hemorrhage, placenta previa, and cervical insufficiency (Hurst, 2014; Spiegel, 2020).

599
01:18:04,768 --> 01:18:09,355
Several cases of spontaneous uterine rupture have been described .

600
01:18:09,355 --> 01:18:14,618
Tissue fragility makes episiotomy repair and cesarean delivery difficult.

601
01:18:14,618 --> 01:18:20,780
A maternal and fetal death from spontaneous rupture of the right iliac artery has been reported .

602
01:18:20,780 --> 01:18:30,180
In another case, a newborn was found to have multiple congenital skull fractures and intracranial hemorrhage caused by Ehlers-Danlos type VIIC .

603
01:18:30,180 --> 01:18:33,468
# Osteogenesis Imperfecta.

604
01:18:33,468 --> 01:18:41,155
This disorder has a prevalence of 1 case in 20,000 births for type I and 1 case in 60,000 for type II .

605
01:18:41,155 --> 01:18:51,530
It is characterized by brittle bones, and affected patients often have blue sclerae, hearing loss, multiple prior bone fractures, and dental abnormalities.

606
01:18:51,700 --> 01:18:58,975
There are up to 15 subtypes based on the causative gene and clinical picture, which ranges from mild to severe .

607
01:18:58,925 --> 01:19:05,512
Genetic inheritance includes autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, and sporadic patterns.

608
01:19:05,512 --> 01:19:11,725
Type I is the mildest form, and the typical mutation affects the COL1A1 gene .

609
01:19:11,725 --> 01:19:14,862
Type II is typically lethal in utero.

610
01:19:14,862 --> 01:19:21,500
Women with osteogenesis imperfecta, most commonly type I, may have successful pregnancies.

611
01:19:21,500 --> 01:19:35,425
However, risks include fractures, complications related to scoliosis with restrictive lung disease, micrognathia, brittle teeth, an unstable cervical spine, uterine rupture, and cephalopelvic disproportion.

612
01:19:35,425 --> 01:19:49,787
A retrospective cohort of 295 women with osteogenesis imperfecta found greater risks of antepartum hemorrhage, placental abruption, fetal-growth restriction, congenital malformations, and preterm birth .

613
01:19:49,787 --> 01:19:55,925
It is not unusual for affected women to enter pregnancy with 20 to 30 prior fractures.

614
01:19:55,925 --> 01:20:03,712
Most require minimal treatment other than management of the fractures and consideration of bisphosphonates to decrease bone loss.

615
01:20:03,712 --> 01:20:12,437
Depending on the type of osteogenesis imperfecta, the fetus may be affected and may also suffer fractures in utero or during delivery.

616
01:20:12,437 --> 01:20:19,525
Preimplantation genetic testing and prenatal diagnosis is available in many situations, if desired.

617
01:20:19,525 --> 01:20:23,425
In utero stem cell therapy is being evaluated .

